course: biology
lesson: 173
title: Coral reef partners and tricksters: clownfish, cleaners, mimics and puffers
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A coral reef is a busy town where many animals make deals: you help me and I help you, like two friends where one carries the shopping and the other opens the doors.
explain: A clownfish lives safely among the stinging tentacles of a sea anemone and chases away fish that would nibble it, a little cleaner fish swims into a big fish's open mouth to pick it clean, and a nearly blind shrimp digs a home that a goby fish guards.
explain: Other reef animals win by tricks: a fake eye near the tail, a fish that swells into a spiky ball, an octopus that pretends to be a lionfish and then a sea snake.
explain: Bright colours on a reef often mean the opposite of hiding: a glowing sea slug or a lionfish with spread fins is saying keep away, I taste bad or I sting.
fact: Clownfish live among the tentacles of sea anemones without getting stung; the clownfish chases away fish that eat anemones, and the anemone protects the clownfish, which rarely swims far from it.
fact: Cleaner wrasse eat parasites (tiny animals that live on other animals and feed off them) and dead skin from bigger fish, even from inside their mouths; in some experiments they seemed to pass a mirror test, though scientists still argue about what that shows.
fact: A nearly blind shrimp, with ten legs like other shrimps, digs and cleans a burrow, and a goby fish keeps watch at the entrance; neither could live as safely alone.
fact: The mimic octopus copies the shapes and movements of dangerous animals such as lionfish and sea snakes; it was first noticed in 1998 off Indonesia.
fact: Many reef fish have a false eye spot near the tail, so a hunter strikes at the wrong end while the fish darts away.
fact: Pufferfish swallow water to swell into a ball too big to eat, and a male pufferfish draws beautiful circles in the sand on the sea floor to attract a female.
fact: Groupers shake their heads at moray eels to invite them to hunt together; the eel goes into cracks and the grouper catches fish that flee into open water.
fact: The trumpetfish hides by swimming glued to the side of a larger harmless fish, and it also hangs head down among soft corals looking like one more stick.
fact: Green turtles visit cleaning stations where fish pick algae off their shells, and as adults they eat plants, which gives their fat a green colour.
fact: Sea slugs called nudibranchs are bright to warn that they taste bad or sting, and some keep algae inside them and make food from sunlight.
fact: Lionfish have venomous spines and hunt small fish; where they have spread far from their home seas and few hunters eat them, divers hunt them and chefs cook them to help reefs.
exercise: How does a clownfish live in a stinging anemone? | It lives among the tentacles without getting stung | It eats the stinging tentacles first, so nothing is left to sting | It wears a shell for protection | It only visits when the anemone sleeps
exercise: What does the anemone get from its clownfish? | The clownfish chases away fish that eat anemones | The clownfish brings it little bits of gold from the reef | The clownfish paints it orange so it looks prettier | Nothing at all
exercise: What does a cleaner wrasse eat? | Parasites and dead skin from bigger fish | Only bits of living coral | Sea grass | The big fish's loose teeth, which they chew on for weeks
exercise: Why are scientists still arguing about cleaner wrasse and the mirror test? | They disagree about what passing the test really shows | No wrasse ever saw a mirror in the whole test, so it was cancelled | The test was done on land | Wrasse broke all the mirrors
exercise: How do a goby and a shrimp help each other? | The shrimp digs the burrow and the goby keeps watch | The goby digs and the shrimp keeps watch for it | They fight for the burrow every day until one leaves for good | The shrimp carries the goby on its back
exercise: What does the mimic octopus do? | It copies the shapes of dangerous animals like lionfish | It copies the voices of whales | It changes into a real fish whenever it likes | It hides inside a borrowed shell all its life, like a hermit crab
exercise: When was the mimic octopus first noticed? | In 1998 | Back in 1798 | Back in 1898 | Last month
exercise: Why do some reef fish have a fake eye spot near the tail? | So a hunter strikes at the wrong end | To see behind them while they swim forwards | To scare their own babies into staying close | To look prettier to the divers who visit
exercise: How does a pufferfish become too big to eat? | It swallows water and swells into a ball | It grows very fast in one day | It sticks to other fish | It blows all the air out of its gills and goes flat like a pancake
exercise: What does a male pufferfish draw in the sand? | Beautiful circles to attract a female | Maps of the reef, so it can find its way home | Pictures of sharks to scare off other fish | Nothing, a fish cannot draw anything at all
exercise: How does a grouper invite a moray eel to hunt? | It shakes its head at the eel | It bites the eel's tail to wake it | It sings a little song to the eel | It brings it some food first as a present
exercise: How does a trumpetfish hide? | It swims glued to the side of a bigger harmless fish | It buries itself deep in the sand all day long | It turns invisible | It swims only at the surface, where the sun hides it from the sharks below
exercise: Why do green turtles visit cleaning stations? | Fish pick algae off their shells | To lay their eggs in a safe spot | To eat the little cleaner fish | To sleep in the anemones
exercise: Why is the fat of adult green turtles green? | They eat plants as adults | They drink green water | They are painted at birth | They eat green fish
exercise: Why are many sea slugs brightly coloured? | To warn that they taste bad or sting | To hide in the sand | To keep warm | Because they are always sick, and the colour shows their illness
exercise: How do some sea slugs make food from sunlight? | They keep algae inside their bodies | They have leaves growing on their backs like plants | They eat sunbeams that shine down through the water | They lie on the beach at low tide to sunbathe
exercise: What is dangerous about a lionfish? | It has venomous spines | It has huge teeth like a shark | It can electrocute you with a zap | It spits poison in your eyes
test: In Finding Nemo, Nemo swims far out into the open sea. What do real clownfish usually do? | They stay very close to their anemone for safety | They swim across whole oceans | They live without any home | They travel with the sea turtles every year, all the way to the beaches
test: «a tiny fish just swam INTO a big fish's mouth and came out?? how» What is going on? | It is a cleaner fish picking off parasites, and the big fish lets it | The big fish was asleep | The tiny fish was stealing the big fish's teeth | It was a fish being eaten that escaped by magic
test: «octopus n a shrimp in the burrow, how many legs n arms total» | 18 | 16 | 12 | 10
test: «2 octopuses, 3 pufferfish. how many arms» | 16 | 8 | 21 | 10
test: What would happen if the goby left the shrimp's burrow? | The nearly blind shrimp would have no guard to warn it of danger | The shrimp would dig faster | The shrimp would see better | Nothing, because the shrimp has perfect eyes and sees danger first itself
test: «lol that sea slug is so bright, it's begging to be eaten» What is right? | No, bright colours warn that it tastes bad or stings | Yes, bright colours make it extra tasty, like sweets | Yes, it wants to be eaten | No, it is bright because it is made of plastic
test: A shark bites at a small fish's tail, where a big round spot is, and the fish shoots away the other way. What happened? | The fake eye spot fooled the shark about which end was the head | The fish was too hard to bite | The shark was not hungry | The spot is a real eye that saw the shark coming from behind
test: In a cartoon a pufferfish blows up with air like a balloon and floats away. What is real? | It swallows water to swell up, and it stays in the water | It always fills up with air and floats away into the sky | It explodes after puffing | It cannot puff up at all
test: «that eel n grouper are mates?? they're fish ffs» Is it true they hunt together? | Yes, the grouper shakes its head to invite the eel and they hunt as a team | No, eels and groupers always fight | No, groupers eat eels | Yes, but only in aquariums
test: «saw an octopus turn into a lionfish then a snake on youtube, fake right» What is right? | It is real: the mimic octopus copies dangerous animals | It is fake, octopuses cannot change shape | It was a real lionfish that turned into a snake | Octopuses only copy rocks
test: Divers spear lionfish and restaurants cook them. Why is this helpful in places where lionfish spread? | Lionfish eat many small reef fish and have few hunters there | Lionfish are the reef's only cleaners, so the fish get sick without them | Lionfish are endangered | It makes the water warmer
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the relationship between the clownfish and the sea anemone? | Each confers protection upon the other, the fish repelling predators of the anemone | The clownfish consumes the anemone gradually | The anemone stings the clownfish to death | The two organisms are entirely indifferent to each other and never interact in any manner
exam: Identify the principal function of the false eye spot situated near the tail of certain reef fish. | To mislead a predator as to the position of the head | To enable the fish to perceive objects behind it | To attract the attention of divers | To absorb sunlight for the purpose of warmth
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the mimic octopus. | It imitates the form and movement of dangerous animals such as lionfish and sea snakes | It transforms itself into a genuine fish | It reproduces the vocal calls of whales | It was described by naturalists in antiquity
exam: Ascertain the means by which the pufferfish renders itself too large to be consumed. | Water is swallowed so that the body is distended into a sphere | Its skeleton is extended by additional bones | Air is expelled forcibly through its gills | It adheres to larger fish for concealment
